WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
How does a nerve signal travel fast down a thin axon?

2
00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.760
Think of a bare axon as a leaky garden hose.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.060
Current leaks out across its membrane at every point.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:17.040
So the action potential has to be rebuilt on every patch it reaches.

5
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:20.780
That is slow. Patch after patch, the signal creeps.

6
00:00:21.833 --> 00:00:23.453
The answer is to wrap it.

7
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:25.233
The wrapping is called myelin.

8
00:00:25.973 --> 00:00:27.953
Myelin is membrane from a glial cell.

9
00:00:28.473 --> 00:00:31.233
In the brain, that cell is an oligodendrocyte.

10
00:00:31.593 --> 00:00:33.373
In a nerve, it is a Schwann cell.

11
00:00:33.993 --> 00:00:37.033
The cell winds its membrane many times around the axon.

12
00:00:37.593 --> 00:00:41.493
The turns are squeezed until almost no cytoplasm is left between them.

13
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:44.997
Think of many thin walls stacked in a row.

14
00:00:45.937 --> 00:00:47.317
Together they make leaking much harder.

15
00:00:48.097 --> 00:00:52.117
Membranes stacked in series raise the resistance across the axon's wall.

16
00:00:52.697 --> 00:00:54.437
They also lower its capacitance.

17
00:00:54.857 --> 00:00:57.577
So current entering at one spot travels farther.

18
00:00:58.137 --> 00:00:59.997
And it charges the membrane ahead faster.

19
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.800
The wrap is not continuous.

20
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.200
Short gaps lie between the sheaths.

21
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.640
These gaps are the nodes of Ranvier.

22
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.680
Each node is a short stretch of bare axon.

23
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.960
Each wrapped stretch is an internode.

24
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.080
The axon packs its voltage-gated sodium channels into the nodes.

25
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.060
They are scarce under the sheath.

26
00:01:19.983 --> 00:01:22.803
Now follow the signal along the wrapped axon.

27
00:01:23.343 --> 00:01:26.263
The action potential is regenerated only at the nodes.

28
00:01:27.123 --> 00:01:30.063
Between nodes, current spreads quietly under the insulation.

29
00:01:30.743 --> 00:01:32.023
It reaches the next node.

30
00:01:32.523 --> 00:01:34.003
There the signal is rebuilt again.

31
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:37.123
So the signal seems to leap from one node to the next.

32
00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:40.903
This is saltatory conduction, from the Latin for to jump.

33
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:43.087
The gain is large.

34
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:47.047
In a bare axon, speed rises with the square root of diameter.

35
00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:51.347
In a myelinated fiber, speed rises in proportion to diameter.

36
00:01:52.027 --> 00:01:55.187
The largest mammalian fibers reach tens of meters per second.

37
00:01:55.647 --> 00:01:59.487
A classic estimate is about six meters per second for each micrometer.

38
00:02:00.267 --> 00:02:03.467
Thin bare fibers conduct at about one meter per second or less.

39
00:02:03.467 --> 00:02:07.287
Wrap the axon, and the signal jumps from gap to gap.
